window.docContents[96].resolve({"/Appendix/#section-chapter-12-algebraic-graph-theory-results":{"contents":"* aut_pathNat₁_eq_one / card_aut_pathNat₁ / not_isVertexTransitive_pathNat₁:\nthe infinite path pathNat₁ is asymmetric, i.e. its only automorphism is the\nidentity, so it is not vertex-transitive.* aut_cycleGraph: every automorphism of C[n + 2] is a rotation, or a rotation\nfollowed by the reflection.* trace_adjMatrix_pow_eq_sum_card_walk: the trace of Aᵏ is the total number of\nclosed walks of length k.* adjMatrix_submatrix_eq_iff: a permutation of the vertices is an automorphism iff\nit preserves the adjacency matrix.* IsVertexTransitive.adjMatrix_pow_apply_self: in a vertex-transitive graph the\ndiagonal entries of each power of the adjacency matrix are all equal, so every\nvertex lies on the same number of closed walks of each length.* IsVertexTransitive.card_walk_self_eq_trace_div: in a vertex-transitive graph on\nn vertices each vertex lies on tr(Aᵏ) / n closed walks of length k.* Iso.charpoly_adjMatrix / Iso.spectrum_adjMatrix: isomorphic graphs have\nadjacency matrices with the same characteristic polynomial and the same spectrum.* adjMatrix_mycielskian_mulVec_mycielskianLift /\nmem_spectrum_adjMatrix_mycielskian: if x ≠ 0 is an eigenvector of G with\neigenvalue ξ whose entries sum to zero, then mycielskianLift φ x and\nmycielskianLift ψ x are eigenvectors of mμ[G] with eigenvalues φ * ξ and\nψ * ξ, where φ and ψ are the golden ratio and its conjugate.* IsRegularOfDegree.finrank_eigenspace_adjMatrix: in a d-regular graph the\nmultiplicity of the eigenvalue d is the number of connected components.* IsSRGWith.petersenGraph: the Petersen graph is strongly regular with parameters\n(10, 3, 0, 1).* HypercubeVert.hammingDist_equivPi: HypercubeVert.equivPi identifies\nHypercubeVert.hammingDist with Mathlib's hammingDist.* edist_hypercubeHamming: the graph distance in the Hamming cube is the Hamming\ndistance.* hammingDist_aut / hammingNorm_aut: automorphisms of the Hamming cube preserve\nHamming distance (and weight, if they fix 0).* aut_eq_id_of_fix_zero_and_singles: an automorphism of the Hamming cube fixing\n0 and all basis vectors is the identity.* card_aut_hypercube: the hypercube Q[n] has exactly 2 ^ n * n.factorial\nautomorphisms.* copyCount_top: the unlabelled copies of the complete graph on W in G are the\ncliques of G with Fintype.card W vertices; so G.copyCount K[s] counts\ns-cliques.* labelledCopyCount_eq_copyCount_mul_labelledCopyCount: the number of labelled\ncopies of a finite graph A in B is the number of unlabelled copies times the\nnumber of automorphisms of A.* labelledCopyCount_cycleGraph: the number of labelled copies of C[n + 3] in a\ngraph is 2 * (n + 3) times the number of unlabelled copies.* labelledCopyCount_hypercube / copyCount_hypercube: the number of labelled\n(resp. unlabelled) copies of Q[k] in Q[n] is 2 ^ n * n.descFactorial k (resp.\nn.choose k * 2 ^ (n - k)).* edist_subcubeCopy: canonical subcube copies are isometric embeddings.* copy_hypercube_adj_iff / card_embedding_hypercube: every copy of Q[k] in\nQ[n] is induced, so there are 2 ^ n * n.descFactorial k embeddings\nQ[k] ↪g Q[n].\n\nMathlib theorems surveyed in this chapter:\n\n* adjMatrix_pow_apply_eq_card_walk: the (u, v) entry of the n-th power of the\nadjacency matrix is the number of walks of length n from u to v.* IsRegularOfDegree.mem_spectrum_adjMatrix: a d-regular graph has eigenvalue d.* card_connectedComponent_eq_finrank_ker_toLin'_lapMatrix: the number of connected\ncomponents of a finite graph is the dimension of the kernel of its Laplacian.* IsSRGWith.param_eq: the parameters of a strongly regular graph satisfy\nk * (k - ℓ - 1) = (n - k - 1) * μ.* IsSRGWith.ediam_eq_two: a strongly regular graph with μ ≠ 0 that is not\ncomplete has diameter 2.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* isVertexTransitive_mulCayley: the Cayley graph of a group is vertex-transitive.* card_aut_cycleGraph / card_aut_completeGraph: C[n + 3] has 2 * (n + 3)\nautomorphisms and K[n] has n.factorial.* even_degree_of_isVertexTransitive: in a vertex-transitive graph with an odd\nnumber of vertices every degree is even.* isVertexTransitive_kneserGraph: Kneser graphs, and so the Petersen graph, are\nvertex-transitive.* exists_aut_hypercubeHamming_of_adj: the Hamming cube is arc-transitive.* copyCount_completeGraph_cycleGraph: K[n + 3] has\n(n + 3).factorial / (2 * (n + 3)) Hamiltonian cycles.* copyCount_hypercube_cycleGraph_four: Q[n] contains n.choose 2 * 2 ^ (n - 2)\n4-cycles.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Results","header":"Chapter 12: Algebraic Graph Theory (Results)","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-12-algebraic-graph-theory-results"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-computations-with-mycielskians":{"contents":"Recall the iterated Mycielskian graphs (iterMycielskian). In order to do\ncomputations with these graphs we need to provide computable instances of Fintype\nfor their vertices and DecidableRel for adjacency. We do this first for the\nMycielskian of a graph and then for the iterated Mycielskian.\n\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n#source mycielskian -- def mycielskian (G : SimpleGraph V) :\n                    --     SimpleGraph (Option (V ⊕ V)) where\n                    --   Adj := fun x y => match x, y with\n                    --     | some (Sum.inl v), some (Sum.inl w) => G.Adj v w\n                    --     | some (Sum.inl v), some (Sum.inr w) => G.Adj v w\n                    --     | some (Sum.inr v), some (Sum.inl w) => G.Adj v w\n                    --     | none, some (Sum.inr w) => True\n                    --     | some (Sum.inr v), none => True\n                    --     | _, _ => False\n\n\nThe Mycielskian of a graph with decidable adjacency also has decidable adjacency./--\nThe Mycielskian of a graph with decidable adjacency also has decidable adjacency. -/\ninstance mycielskianDecidableRelAdj [DecidableRel G.Adj] : DecidableRel mμ[G].Adj :=\n  fun x y => by\n    rcases x with _ | (_ | _) <;> rcases y with _ | (_ | _)\n       <;> unfold mycielskian <;> infer_instance\n\n\n#source MycielskianVert -- notation: mμᵥ^n V\n\n\nThe vertex type of the n-th iterated Mycielskian of a graph with decidable\nvertex equality also has decidable vertex equality./-- The vertex type of the n-th iterated Mycielskian of a graph with decidable\nvertex equality also has decidable vertex equality. -/\ninstance iterMycielskianDecidableEq (n : ℕ) [DecidableEq V] :\n    DecidableEq (mμᵥ^n V) := by\n  induction n <;> unfold MycielskianVert <;> infer_instance\n\n\nThe vertex type of the n-th iterated Mycielskian of a graph with finitely many\nvertices also has finitely many vertices./-- The vertex type of the n-th iterated Mycielskian of a graph with finitely many\nvertices also has finitely many vertices. -/\ninstance iterMycielskianFintype (n : ℕ) [Fintype V] : Fintype (mμᵥ^n V) := by\n  induction n <;> unfold MycielskianVert <;> infer_instance\n\n\n#source iterMycielskian -- notation: mμ^n G\n\n\nIf G has decidable adjacency then so does the n-th iterated Mycielskian of\nG./-- If G has decidable adjacency then so does the n-th iterated Mycielskian of\nG. -/\ninstance iterMycielskianDecidableRelAdj (n : ℕ) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    DecidableRel (mμ^n G).Adj := by\n  induction n <;> unfold iterMycielskian <;> infer_instance\n\n\nThese instances combine with those from earlier in the chapter. For example, Lean can\nfind a Fintype instance for the edge set of the 4th iterated Mycielskian of\nC[5][2], the 2-uniform blow-up of C[5] (see blowUpN).\n\n#synth Fintype (mμ^4 (C[5][2])).edgeSet -- (mμ^4 C[5][2]).fintypeEdgeSet\n\n\nThe 6th iterated Mycielskian of C[5] has 383 vertices and 7271 edges; it has\nminimum degree 8 and maximum degree 191.\n\n#eval Fintype.card (mμᵥ^6 (Fin 5)) -- 383\n#eval (mμ^6 C[5]).edgeFinset.card -- 7271\n#eval δ(mμ^6 C[5]) -- 8\n#eval Δ(mμ^6 C[5]) -- 191\n\n\nRecall the notation we introduced for the vertices of the iterated Mycielskian. The\nvertex v ₗ is the left copy of v in the Mycielskian of a SimpleGraph V, and\nv ᵣ is its right copy.\n\nRecall also that the Grötzsch graph is the Mycielskian of C[5]. This has decidable\nadjacency so we can reason about it in proofs using decide.\n\nexample : grotzschGraph.Adj 2 ₗ 3 ᵣ := by\n  decide\n\n\nexample : (mμ^7 C[5]).Adj (0 : Fin 5)ₗₗᵣᵣᵣₗₗ (1 : Fin 5)ᵣᵣₗₗₗᵣₗ := by decide\n#eval (mμ^10 C[5]).degree none ᵣₗᵣₗᵣₗᵣ -- 199\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees","header":"4.5. Computations with Mycielskians","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-computations-with-mycielskians"},"/Walks/#section-copy":{"contents":"Having solved the problem of type mismatches involving nil, we now turn to the more\ngeneral problem of type mismatches between arbitrary walks. For example if\np : G.Walk u v₁ and q : G.Walk v₂ w where v₁ and v₂ are provably, but not\ndefinitionally, equal then we can't directly form Walk.append p q since append\nhas a signature that expects compatible walks. If we try to write p.append q we get\na type error.\n\n/- Application type mismatch: The argument\n  q\nhas type\n  G.Walk v₂ w\nbut is expected to have type\n  G.Walk v₁ w\n-/\nexample {v₁ v₂ : V} (p : G.Walk u v₁) (q : G.Walk v₂ w) (h : v₂ = v₁) :\n  G.Walk u w :=  p.append q\n\n\nA quick fix for this is to change the type of q using h : v₂ = v₁ and ▸:\n\nexample {v₁ v₂ : V} (p : G.Walk u v₁) (q : G.Walk v₂ w) (h : v₂ = v₁) :\n  G.Walk u w :=  p.append (h ▸ q)\n\n\nThis changes the type of q without changing the walk itself.\n\nMathlib's Walk.copy packages this cast with a name, so we can state lemmas about\nit, and it lets both endpoints be changed at once.\n\n#source Walk.copy\nexample {v₁ v₂ : V} (p : G.Walk u v₁) (q : G.Walk v₂ w) (h : v₂ = v₁) :\n  G.Walk u w :=  p.append (q.copy h rfl)\n\n\nA simple example of where we need to introduce a Walk.copy is in the proof that\n(p.cons h).tail = p.\n\nOn paper this is completely obvious: Walk.tail is the walk obtained by dropping its\nfirst edge, so (p.cons h).tail first adds and then drops the edge h to the start\nof p. However the type of (p.cons h).tail is G.Walk (p.cons h).snd w while p\nhas type G.Walk v w. The vertex (p.cons h).snd unfolds to p.getVert 0, which is\nprovably but not definitionally equal to v. So the obvious equality does not type\ncheck. Instead we have to state this using Walk.copy. The definition of\ngetVert matches on the walk before the index, so even p.getVert 0 is stuck until\nLean knows whether p is nil or a cons, although both cases give v.\n\n#check tail_cons -- (cons h p).tail = p.copy ⋯ ⋯\n\n\nHaving introduced Walk.copy it's instructive to look at its API. Since\nhu : u = u' and hv : v = v' are proofs of equality, p.copy hu hv is the walk\np itself, retyped as a walk from u' to v'. So every statement about p has a\ncounterpart for p.copy hu hv, saying the same thing. In each case the proof is\nsimply subst_vars followed by rfl.\n\nFor example length_copy, support_copy, edges_copy and isPath_copy.\n\n#check Walk.length_copy  -- (p.copy hu hv).length = p.length\n#check Walk.support_copy -- (p.copy hu hv).support = p.support\n#check Walk.edges_copy   -- (p.copy hu hv).edges = p.edges\n#check Walk.isPath_copy  -- (p.copy hu hv).IsPath ↔ p.IsPath\n\n\nThe subst_vars is essential. As its source shows, copy is just the cast\nhu ▸ hv ▸ p, and like the cast h ▸ v in the disjoint union Σg\n(SimpleGraph.sigma), it is stuck while the endpoints are distinct variables. So not\neven the length of a copied walk can be computed, and rfl fails.\n\n/- error: Tactic rfl failed: The left-hand side\n  (p.copy hu hv).length\nis not definitionally equal to the right-hand side\n  p.length\n-/\nexample {u' v'} (p : G.Walk u v) (hu : u = u') (hv : v = v') :\n    (p.copy hu hv).length = p.length := by rfl\n\n\nSubstituting identifies u' with u and v' with v, so both casts are now along\ndefinitional equations and disappear, after which rfl succeeds. (For the same\nreason copy_rfl_rfl, which copies along rfl proofs, is itself proved by rfl.)\n\nexample {u' v'} (p : G.Walk u v) (hu : u = u') (hv : v = v') :\n    (p.copy hu hv).length = p.length := by\n  subst_vars\n  rfl\n\n\nNotice that the four copy lemmas listed above are all simp lemmas with p.copy on\nthe LHS so that simp will systematically remove .copy when possible by rewriting to\nthe original walk.\n\nAnother class of results involving Walk.copy are those that allow us to manipulate\na copied walk. For example if we add an edge to a copy using cons then this is\nthe same as taking a copy of the equivalent cons walk.\n\n#check Walk.cons_copy --  cons h (p.copy hv hw) = (cons ⋯ p).copy ⋯ hw\n\n\nSome, but certainly not all, such results are simp lemmas. To understand why, we need\nto read the doc-string for Walk.copy, in particular the following comment:\n\nThe simp-normal form is for the copy to be pushed outward. That way calculations\ncan occur within the \"copy context\".\n\nWhat this means is that lemmas involving Walk.copy are only marked as @[simp] if\nthey push the copy outwards. The simp normal form is\n(some_walk_not_involving_copy).copy hv hw. (With copy on the outside, simp can\napply other walk simp lemmas freely to the inner walk without copy interfering.)\n\nFor example cons_copy and append_copy_copy are both good simp lemmas while\ncopy_cons is not.\n\n#source cons_copy --  cons h (p.copy hv hw) = (cons (hv ▸ h) p).copy rfl hw\n\n\n#source append_copy_copy -- (p.copy hu hv) ++ (q.copy hv hw) = (p ++ q).copy hu hw\n\n\n#source copy_cons --  (cons h p).copy hu hw = cons (hu ▸ h) (p.copy rfl hw)\n\n\nTo see what this means in practice, we consider the following concrete example.\nSuppose that simp encounters cons h₁ (cons h₂ (p.copy hv hw)). In this case\ncons_copy fires on the inner walk cons h₂ (p.copy hv hw), rewriting to\ncons h₁ ((cons (hv ▸ h₂) p).copy rfl hw). Now there is again a copy inside a\ncons so cons_copy fires again to give:\n(cons h₁ (cons (hv ▸ h₂) p)).copy rfl hw.\n\nThe copy is now on the outside and the result is in normal form:\n(some_walk).copy rfl hw.\n\nIf the equality proofs were both rfl, then copy_rfl_rfl would fire and eliminate\nthe copy entirely!\n\n#check Walk.copy_rfl_rfl -- (p.copy rfl rfl) = p\n\n\nThe following lemma (the concat analogue of cons_copy from above, not currently\nin Mathlib) is a good simp lemma.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.concat_copy {v w y v' w'} (h : G.Adj w y) (p : G.Walk v' w') (hv : v' = v)\n    (hw : w' = w) : (p.copy hv hw).concat h = (p.concat (hw ▸ h)).copy hv rfl := by\n  subst_vars\n  rfl\n\n\nAs a worked example consider the following situation: we have h₁ : G.Adj a b and\nh₂ : G.Adj c d, as well as walks p : G.Walk u v and q : G.Walk v y; and proofs\nthat u = b, v = x and y = c. Making heavy use of Walk.copy we can construct a\nwalk G.Walk a d that is essentially the incorrectly typed walk\n(cons h₁ (p ++ q)).concat h₂. This walk has length p.length + q.length + 2.\n\nexample {a b c d x y} (hub : u = b) (hvx : v = x) (hyc : y = c)\n    (p : G.Walk u v) (q : G.Walk v y) (h₁ : G.Adj a b) (h₂ : G.Adj c d) :\n    ((cons h₁ ((p.copy hub hvx).append (q.copy hvx hyc))).concat h₂).length\n      = p.length + q.length + 2 := by\n  -- simp would prove this, but it's nice to see the individual steps.\n  rw [append_copy_copy, cons_copy, concat_copy, length_copy, length_concat,\n    length_cons, length_append]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Plumbing: Fixing Type Mismatches","header":"8.5.2. Copy","id":"/Walks/#section-copy"}});